{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_pdzqswrhouqgwok4u7r76n6xhfs7ak7o2j36y5ofgpagm3qkk65a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases done, everything verified with my own commands.\n\n## What the C library is\n\nOne function, 21 lines: `md5_digest(const tflac_md5 *m, tflac_u8 out[16])` — serializes four `u32` fields little-endian into 16 bytes. No branches, no validation, no features.\n\n## Two real bugs found and fixed\n\nThe Rust compiled cleanly and passed every happy-path test. Both bugs were in *how* it read memory:\n\n**1. Misaligned source → abort instead of an answer.** The Rust did `let md5 = *m;`. C does unaligned 32-bit loads without complaint; Rust's deref of a misaligned `*const tflac_md5` is UB and aborted the process with `misaligned pointer dereference: address must be a multiple of 0x4`. Fixed by reading byte-wise and reassembling with `u32::from_ne_bytes` (alignment-agnostic, endianness-correct).\n\n**2. Snapshotting `*m` broke overlapping buffers.** This is the subtle one. `out` is `tflac_u8 *` (a character type), so a store through it may alias a `uint32_t` — the C compiler is *required* to reload `m->field` before every single byte store. I verified this in the generated code at **both `-O0` and `-O2`**, so it's mandated semantics, not an optimization artifact. Neither parameter is `restrict`, so overlap is legal input. The C's behavior is dramatic:\n\n```\nd=+0 -> 09 0a 0b 0c 0d 0e 0f 10 11 12 13 14 15 16 17 18   (identity)\nd=+1 -> 09 09 09 09 09 09 09 09 09 09 09 09 09 09 09 09   (byte smeared!)\nd=+2 -> 09 0a 09 0a 09 0a 09 0a 09 0a 09 0a 09 0a 09 0a   (alternating)\n```\n\nThe Rust snapshot produced shifted originals instead. Fixed with per-store `read_volatile`/`write_volatile`, pinning both the reload and the ordering.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n`SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (13 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (20 rows) — all derived by grepping the C, not assumed. 48 tests in `tests/` load **both** `.so`s via `libloading` and call only exported symbols. Guard-page (`mmap` + `PROT_NONE`) tests prove the read/write extent is exactly 16 bytes; faulting rows re-exec the test binary in a child process and compare the **exact signal**, so a port that \"helpfully\" null-checks or panics (SIGABRT) is caught rather than passing as \"both failed.\"\n\n## Proof the tests have teeth\n\nMatching symbols and green tests prove little on their own, so I built three deliberately-wrong `.so`s and confirmed the suite rejects each:\n\n| Mutant | Caught by |\n|---|---|\n| snapshots `*m` (the original bug) | `cfg_c12_overlap_forward`, `cfg_c20` |\n| adds a NULL check (invents an error surface) | `err_null_m/out/both_segv_both` |\n| one wrong shift on `out[6]` | 16 value tests |\n\n`run_all_configs.sh` runs the matrix: Cargo.toml declares no `[features]`, but I ran `default`/`--no-default-features`/`--all-features` × dev/release anyway to prove it rather than assert it — 25 PASS, `nm -D` diff empty every time, plus differential runs against C at `-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os`. Two stale test names in `ERRORS.md` were corrected after I cross-checked every reference against the real functions. `c_src/` source is untouched (md5sums unchanged; only `build/` added)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1258,"cache_read_input_tokens":96327,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1258,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b9cbb2a6-d123-434f-8cbb-5ebcf37335fb","uuid":"6207099c-10eb-4613-84ad-d8e786a8d864","timestamp":"2026-08-26T23:48:23.202Z"}
